course: dictionary
lesson: 4990
title: Words about where things come from
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: Some words tell you where people and things come from. Indigenous means first known to live in a place, or growing and living naturally there. Read the clues around it: «The kangaroo is indigenous to Australia» tells you the kangaroo lives there naturally, not because people brought it.
explain: Indigenous can also describe people. «About 476 million Indigenous people live today, and many are bringing back their languages, rights and lands.» The clues are languages, rights and lands: these people belong to a place that was theirs long before others arrived.
explain: Always test your meaning in the sentence. Swap in «natural to a place» and check: «The kangaroo is natural to Australia» still makes sense, so you are on the right track. Science texts often set indigenous against invasive, which means brought in from somewhere else.
fact: Indigenous means first known to live in a place, or growing or living naturally there.
fact: With a capital I, Indigenous often names peoples, such as Māori in New Zealand, Inuit in Canada, and Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander peoples in Australia.
fact: In science, an indigenous plant or animal is one that was not brought to its place by people.
fact: «Invasive» species are brought to a place from somewhere else, which is why science lessons compare the two words.
fact: Around 476 million Indigenous people live in the world today.
exercise: «The kangaroo is indigenous to Australia.» What does indigenous mean here? | Brought to Australia by ships | Found only in zoos | Very large and fierce | *Grows and lives there naturally
exercise: «About 476 million Indigenous people live today.» Which meaning fits Indigenous people? | Scientists who study the past | *The first peoples known to live in a place, and their descendants | Visitors who came from far away | Animals that live in the wild
exercise: Mia says a rabbit brought to Australia by people is indigenous. Is she right? | Yes, because it lives there now | *No, it was brought by people, so it is not indigenous | Yes, because it is small and quick | No, because rabbits cannot live in Australia
test: «Many Indigenous peoples believe that nature has spirits.» Who does Indigenous name here? | Only people who follow Christianity | Scientists studying spirits | Wild animals | *The first peoples of a place and their descendants
test: In a science lesson, which is the best meaning of indigenous? | Brought to a place by people | Dangerous to other animals | Found in a museum | *Growing or living naturally in a place
exam: Why is «the kangaroo is indigenous to Australia» different from «a rabbit was brought to Australia by people»? | It has a longer tail and is faster | Both were brought to Australia by people | *The kangaroo lives there naturally, while the rabbit was brought there by people
